WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.400
Andrés Muschietti's "It: Chapter 2"

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.320
wraps up the events of the
2017 box-office smash "It."

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.020
At nearly three hours long,
it provides plenty of scares

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.500
and one hilarious
performance from Bill Hader.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.462
But did you spot these references

00:00:19.462 --> 00:00:21.753
to other Stephen King adaptations

00:00:21.753 --> 00:00:23.761
or callbacks to the first film?

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.563
And, yes, spoilers are coming.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.860
The movie is set in 2016,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.330
27 years after the
events of the first film.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.963
The book, "It" by Stephen King,

00:00:37.963 --> 00:00:41.660
is set in 1958 and 1985,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.783
so there was some deviation
from the original novel.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.130
The movie opens with the
homophobic attack on Adrian.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.750
This is featured in the novel,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.600
but not in the 1990 miniseries.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.111
King based the event on

00:00:56.111 --> 00:01:00.240
the real-life 1984
murder of Charlie Howard,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.323
a gay man attacked and thrown

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:05.473
from the State Street
Bridge in Bangor, Maine.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.760
Early in the film, we're
introduced to the now adult

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.420
Bill Denbrough on the
set of the adaptation

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.177
of his book, "Attic Room."

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.680
The director of "Attic Room"?
It's Peter Bogdanovich,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.290
the director of "The Last Picture Show."

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.840
A King connection, the author
makes repeated references

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.600
to "The Last Picture Show"
in his novel "Lisey's Story,"

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.253
as character Scott Landon
frequently watches the film.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.779
Bill also talks about
sliding under the stage door

00:01:37.779 --> 00:01:39.245
like Indiana Jones.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.530
Bill's Beverly-lookalike
wife in this scene

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.483
is played by Jessica Chastain's
real-life BFF, Jess Weixler.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.060
When now adult Beverly is
reunited with Mike and Ben,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.870
we can see the scars on her arm

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.183
from her abusive husband, Tom Rogan.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.820
Beverly goes to visit her childhood home.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.350
It's inhabited by Mrs. Kersh,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.580
as the name on the door changes
briefly from Marsh to Kersh.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.480
King named the character Mrs.
Kersh after Irvin Kershner,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.630
the director of "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.770
The 1980 movie was shot in the same studio

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.400
at the same time as "The Shining,"

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.880
and King even visited the set.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.030
Something not really
featured in the 2017 film

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.430
is Beverly's nightmares
about Hansel and Gretel.

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:33.390
Mrs. Kersh is reminiscent of the witch

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.470
in the Germanic folk story,

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.660
making a comment about the hot oven

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.020
and welcoming Beverly inside for tea,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.920
just like the children in the fairy tale

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.493
are welcomed in for sweets.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.670
There are a few Pennywise clues

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.970
before Mrs. Kersh
transforms into the villain.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.150
The actress who plays
Mrs. Kersh, Joan Gregson,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.090
is no stranger to King's
work, having starred in

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.690
ABC's "Storm of the Century" in 1999.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.710
We see shop exteriors
of the town of Derry,

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.100
some of which are named
after real-life places

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.030
in Port Hope, Ontario,
where the movie was filmed.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.900
There is a real-life Queenies Bake Shop.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.123
Fun fact: Queenie was also the
name of King's childhood dog.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.350
Inside Derry's secure institution,

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.870
a cartoon is playing on the TV.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.540
This cartoon is "Tom and
Jerry: The Truce Hurts,"

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.600
depicting a steak falling into a sewer.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.137
The steak floats away just
like Georgie's boat in "It."

00:03:34.627 --> 00:03:36.470
The movie includes lots of flashbacks

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.420
and locations the
characters saw decades ago,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.133
so there are a lot of '80s
references throughout.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.550
The Losers return to their
underground clubhouse.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.943
In a flashback scene, we
get a few '80s references.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:54.400
Cameo's 1986 hit "Word Up" is playing.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.200
There's a poster for the
1987 movie "The Lost Boys,"

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.960
and one of Michael J. Fox

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.650
from his 1987 cover of Rolling Stone.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.040
Richie revisits the
arcade where he had played

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.760
Street Fighter, the classic 1980s game.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.750
On the wall behind him is a poster

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.690
for 1998's "You've Got Mail."

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.850
This is the second time Meg
Ryan pops up in the movie,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.890
as you might recall in the opening scenes

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.677
Adrian shouts to the
bullies, "Meg Ryan called.

00:04:22.677 --> 00:04:25.010
She wants her wig back."

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.680
Ben hides in a locker from Pennywise.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.300
Behind him, on the locker wall,

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.113
we see a poster of Danny
from New Kids on the Block.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.470
Here's where we also get a direct quote

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.950
from the 1990 miniseries.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.900
Pennywise: Kiss me, fat boy.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.820
Narrator: The turtle
from the books pops up

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.350
in several places in the movie.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.870
There's a drawing of a turtle on the desk

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.940
in Ben's classroom, a statue
of one outside Ben's house,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:51.520
and also an engraving of one

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.510
on the door to Pennywise's lair.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.110
King makes a cameo in the movie,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.010
much like he has done in
other adaptations of his work.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.130
This time, he's an antiques dealer

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.853
wearing a Neil Young T-shirt.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.400
An old license plate near the counter

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.841
is the CQB 241 license

00:05:12.841 --> 00:05:15.249
on the 1958 Plymouth Fury

00:05:15.249 --> 00:05:17.457
in 1983's "Christine."

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.880
In the "It" novel, Henry
Bowers escapes from the asylum

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.260
in a cherry-red 1958 Plymouth Fury.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.670
King makes a joke at his own expense.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.920
Bill asks King's character

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.820
if he wants him to sign
the book that he's reading,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.540
to which King replies no,
as the ending was bad.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.000
The book's cover has
all the visual hallmarks

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.880
of a King novel.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.720
This parallel between
criticisms of King's work

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.370
and Bill's books having bad endings

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.763
crops up a few times in the movie.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:53.130
The store, Secondhand Rose,
is the same from the novel

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:58.130
and 1994's "Insomnia," Secondhand
Rose, Secondhand Clothes.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:02.810
Speaking of cameos, look
out for Andrés Muschietti

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.662
as a customer in the drugstore

00:06:04.662 --> 00:06:07.163
when Eddie goes to pick
up his prescription.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.080
Eddie's wife, Myra Kaspbrak,
is played by the same actress

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.000
who played his mother, Sonia.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.290
Guess he never really
escaped his mom after all.

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.870
One of adult Ben's boardroom colleagues

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.049
is none other than Brandon Crane,

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:26.473
who played the kid Ben
in the 1990 miniseries.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:31.060
In a scene where Bill runs
through a fairground fun house,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.700
the swinging clowns he
ran past are dressed up

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:37.683
just like Tim Curry's
1990 version of Pennywise.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.670
As the gang battles Pennywise,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.950
he turns into a monster reminiscent

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.620
of the spider-legged alien
in 1982's "The Thing."

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.320
This time, it's young Stanley's head

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.210
with arachnid legs growing out of it.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.053
This is accompanied by a
quote from "The Thing."

00:06:56.053 --> 00:06:58.313
Palmer: You've gotta be f---ing kidding.

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:01.440
Narrator: When Beverly struggles for air

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.650
in the blood-soaked toilet cubicle,

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.260
a variety of faces try
to edge through the door.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.980
One of them says, "Here's Johnny,"

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.917
in reference to "The Shining."

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.570
Another 1980s movie, 1989's
"Nightmare on Elm Street 5,"

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.683
is playing in the movie
theater in the closing shots.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:24.040
In "It: Chapter 2,"
the defeat of Pennywise

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.260
is almost like a late-series
Freddy Krueger movie:

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.463
surreal and CGI-heavy.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.500
Bill receives a phone call from Mike

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.753
at the end of the movie.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.090
In these scenes, he is reading a copy

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.370
of "A History of Old Derry"
by Branson Buddinger.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.330
You may recall this book
from the first movie

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.670
as the one Ben reads in Derry Library.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.670
In fact, the room Bill
is in looks quite similar

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.200
to the office in the final
scene of "Stand by Me,"

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.530
where Richard Dreyfuss, as the writer,

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.673
types the movie's final
scenes into his computer.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.420
Did you spot anything else?

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:04.753
Comment below.

